|> Exercise
    handle=_inverse_graph_introduction_

    |> CodexSuggestion

        Let

        $$
        f^{-1}
        $$

        stand for the “inverse” of a function $f$
        for which such an inverse exists. 
        This means that $f$ has the kind of graph
        below, in which distinct $x$'s are mapped to
        distinct $y$'s:

        |> Image
            src=images/e6_f_inverse_distinct.svg

        This means that we can invert the flow
        of the function, treating $y$'s as inputs, and recovering a
        unique $x$ for each $y$ that is an output of $f$—this
        “inverse flow” function is what we write as “$f^{-1}$”:


        |> Image
            src=images/e6_f_inverse_reversed.svg

        The picture above shows $f^{-1}$ with inputs on the vertical axis
        and outputs on the horizontal axis. What would its “ordinary” graph
        look like, with inputs on the horizontal axis and outputs on the vertical axis?

    |> Solution
        |> CodexSuggestion

            Each point $(x,y)$ on the graph of $f$ becomes a point $(y,x)$
            on the ordinary graph of $f^{-1}$. The two graphs are reflections
            of each other across the line $y=x$:

            |> Image
                src=images/e6_f_inverse_upright.svg
